          },
          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>A patient with benign prostatic hyperplasia needs pharmacological treatment. DHT</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A patient with benign prostatic hyperplasia needs pharmacological treatment. DHT — which has 5× greater androgen receptor affinity than testosterone — is the dominant androgen driving prostatic growth. Which drug target would most specifically reduce DHT-mediated prostatic enlargement?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Androgen receptor blockade</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>AR antagonists (e.g. flutamide) block both testosterone and DHT at the receptor. While effective, this is not specific to DHT — it also abolishes testosterone's beneficial effects in muscle and bone.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>The enzyme that converts testosterone to DHT in target tissues</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. Inhibiting 5α-reductase specifically reduces DHT production in the prostate while preserving testosterone's actions in the hypothalamus and skeletal muscle.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Hypothalamic GnRH receptors</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>GnRH suppression eliminates all gonadal androgens — this non-specific approach is used in advanced prostate cancer, not as first-line treatment for BPH.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Aromatase</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Aromatase converts testosterone to oestradiol, not to DHT. Inhibiting aromatase would not address DHT-driven prostatic growth.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Recall that DHT is the dominant androgen in the prostate because of its 5× greater AR affinity. The most specific pharmacological target is the enzyme (5α-reductase) that converts testosterone to DHT in prostatic tissue."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Since DHT — not testosterone — drives prostatic growth due to its 5× greater AR affinity, inhibiting 5α-reductase (e.g. finasteride, dutasteride) specifically reduces prostatic DHT while sparing testosterone's actions in muscle and hypothalamus."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
