          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>A 30-year-old woman on a combined oral contraceptive misses two consecutive pill</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A 30-year-old woman on a combined oral contraceptive misses two consecutive pills mid-cycle. Which pharmacological consequence is the most immediate threat to contraceptive efficacy?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Cervical mucus thins, allowing sperm penetration</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Cervical mucus thickening is a secondary progestogenic mechanism. While clinically relevant, it is not the primary contraceptive action of combined pills.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Endometrial atrophy reverses, favouring implantation</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Endometrial changes are a tertiary mechanism of hormonal contraception. Reversal takes days, and this is not the most immediate threat when pills are missed.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>FSH and LH suppression is released, permitting follicular development and potential ovulation</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. The primary mechanism of COCs is prevention of ovulation via negative feedback on the HPO axis. Missing pills releases FSH and LH suppression — follicular development can resume within 48 hours, with the risk of ovulation.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Withdrawal bleeding begins immediately, flushing the uterine lining</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Withdrawal bleeding requires sustained hormone withdrawal over several days. It is a consequence, not a mechanism — and does not represent the primary threat to contraceptive efficacy.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "The video described three pharmacological targets of hormonal contraceptives. The primary mechanism — and the one most immediately compromised by missed pills — is prevention of ovulation through FSH/LH suppression via HPO-axis negative feedback."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "The primary mechanism of COCs is suppression of ovulation through oestrogen–progestogen negative feedback on the HPO axis. Missing pills releases FSH and LH, allowing follicular development and possible ovulation — the most critical failure mode."
                }
              ],
