          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>A 28-year-old woman with infertility presents with elevated serum prolactin, gal</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A 28-year-old woman with infertility presents with elevated serum prolactin, galactorrhoea, and anovulatory cycles. Which drug class directly targets the underlying pathophysiology causing her anovulation?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Insulin sensitisers (e.g. metformin)</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Metformin targets insulin resistance in PCOS-related anovulation. It has no direct effect on prolactin secretion and would not address this patient's pathophysiology.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Aromatase inhibitors (e.g. letrozole)</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Letrozole induces ovulation by reducing oestrogen-mediated negative feedback, but it does not treat the underlying hyperprolactinaemia that is suppressing GnRH pulsatility.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Exogenous gonadotrophins (FSH/hMG)</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Gonadotrophins can stimulate follicular development directly, but they bypass the problem rather than treating the cause. Once prolactin is normalised, spontaneous ovulation often resumes.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Dopamine agonists (e.g. bromocriptine, cabergoline)</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. Elevated prolactin suppresses pulsatile GnRH secretion, leading to low FSH/LH and anovulation. Dopamine agonists activate D2 receptors on pituitary lactotrophs, reducing prolactin and restoring normal HPO-axis function.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Each cause of anovulatory infertility maps to a specific drug target: PCOS → insulin sensitisers + ovulation inducers; hyperprolactinaemia → dopamine agonists; hypogonadotrophic hypogonadism → exogenous gonadotrophins. Match the drug to the underlying cause."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Hyperprolactinaemia suppresses GnRH pulsatility → low FSH/LH → anovulation. Dopamine agonists (bromocriptine or cabergoline) treat the cause by lowering prolactin, restoring HPO-axis function, and permitting spontaneous ovulation."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
